<div class="container prose">
  <h2>Understanding Stress</h2>
  <p>Stress is your body's alarm system. In small doses, it's useful — it sharpens focus and mobilizes energy. The problem arises when the alarm never stops ringing. Chronic stress keeps cortisol elevated, weakens immunity, disrupts sleep, and wears down every system in the body.</p>
  
  <p>The goal isn't to eliminate stress (that's impossible and unnecessary). The goal is <strong>resilience</strong> — the ability to activate when needed and fully recover when the threat passes.</p>

  <h2>Natural Approaches That Work</h2>
  
  <div class="stress-grid">
    <div class="stress-item">
      <h3>🫁 Physiological Sigh</h3>
      <p>Two quick inhales through the nose, followed by a long slow exhale through the mouth. This is the fastest known way to reduce acute stress — it works on your brainstem directly, bypassing thought.</p>
      <p class="protocol"><strong>Protocol:</strong> 5–10 cycles when you feel stress spiking. No tools needed. Works anywhere.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="stress-item">
      <h3>🌿 Aconitum Napellus (Aconite)</h3>
      <p>The classic remedy for sudden, intense fear or anxiety — the kind that hits fast and feels like the ground dropped out. Useful after a shock, bad news, or acute panic moment.</p>
      <p class="protocol"><strong>Typical use:</strong> 30C, 3–5 pellets, once. Repeat only if the initial state returns unchanged.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="stress-item">
      <h3>🌿 Gelsemium</h3>
      <p>For the specific pattern of anticipatory anxiety — the dread before an event, the weakness and dizziness that comes with overthinking. "I can't do this" energy.</p>
      <p class="protocol"><strong>Typical use:</strong> 6C or 30C, 3 pellets, 20 minutes before the stressful event.</p>
    </div>
    
    <div class="stress-item">
      <h3>🌿 Arsenicum Album</h3>
      <p>For anxiety that comes with restlessness and a need to be busy — pacing, fidgeting, inability to sit still. Often accompanied by heightened sensitivity to germs or disorder.</p>
      <p class="protocol"><strong>Typical use:</strong> 30C, 3 pellets at the first sign of the pattern. Usually once is enough.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stress-item">
      <h3>🧘 Progressive Muscle Relaxation</h3>
      <p>Systematically tensing and releasing each muscle group from toes to forehead. Teaches your body what "relaxed" actually feels like when you've forgotten.</p>
      <p class="protocol"><strong>Protocol:</strong> 10–15 minutes daily, ideally before sleep. Audio guides available to subscribers.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="stress-item">
      <h3>🌲 Nature Immersion</h3>
      <p>The Black Hills aren't just scenery — they're medicine. Research consistently shows that 20+ minutes in a natural environment reduces cortisol, lowers blood pressure, and improves attention.</p>
      <p class="protocol"><strong>Protocol:</strong> Daily walk in nature, no phone. Weekly longer immersion if possible.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>The Stress Cycle</h2>
  <p>Stress isn't just mental — it's physical. Your body produces hormones and tensed muscles that need to be <em>completed</em>. A stress response without a completion cycle (movement, shaking, deep breath, emotional release) gets stuck.</p>
  
  <p>Think of it this way: your body prepared to fight or run. If you didn't, that energy is still in the system. The practices above help complete that cycle so your nervous system can return to baseline.</p>

